<h2>A short guided breathing exercise</h2>
<p>Cortisol+ includes an animated breathing exercise with prompts, a cycle counter and an option to end early. It offers a structured pause you can follow on screen. It does not test cortisol before or after the session.</p>
<h2>What is included</h2>
<p>The iPhone exercise reviewed for this guide uses three repeating phases: breathe in, hold and breathe out, each lasting four seconds. Eight cycles take about 96 seconds. The start screen calls this “4-4-4 box breathing”; this implementation does not include a fourth hold after the exhale.</p>
<ol><li>Open the breathing exercise and select <strong>Begin</strong>.</li><li>Follow the expanding and contracting circle and the phase prompts at a pace that is comfortable for you.</li><li>Use <strong>End Early</strong> if you want to stop. The completion screen lets you close the exercise.</li></ol>
<p>This describes the implemented exercise reviewed September 9, 2026. We have removed earlier website claims about four selectable protocols and automatic before-and-after hormone measurements because those claims were not supported by the reviewed implementation.</p>
<h2>What a session result can tell you</h2>
<p>Completing the timer records that you used the exercise. It does not show that stress, heart rate or cortisol changed by a promised amount. If you review a wearable reading afterward, check its timestamp: the latest available sample may predate the exercise.</p>
<p>Notice how the exercise feels without treating a score as proof of success. There is no established Cortisol+ result that every user should reach after one session or several weeks.</p>
<h2>How breathing fits with wearable tracking</h2>
<p>You can keep a note that you tried the exercise and compare it with your own sleep and routine records. This is an observation, not a controlled experiment. A change on the same day cannot establish that breathing caused it.</p>
